Driving adapters (orange) call the core; driven adapters (purple) are called by it, each implementing exactly one port from the diagram above.
Every arrow into the hexagon points **inwards**, and every arrow out of it stops at a port; `main.rs` is the only file that knows a `BuildingStore` is really MongoDB, and swapping in `service/fakes.rs` is what lets the whole core suite run with no database and no network.

### Ports and adapters — who each one is for

A port exists **for the use case that declares it**, not for whoever ends up implementing it. The use case's author writes the trait signature (`service/ports.rs`) to fit their own logic, and everyone downstream must conform to it.

*   **Driving Adapters**: These originate requests for the core.
    *   `api/claims.rs`: Extracts `x-gateway-claims` from incoming HTTP requests, transforming raw header data into the domain's `GatewayClaims`.
    *   `api/buildings.rs`: Contains HTTP handlers that translate external `POST /building` requests into calls to the core's use cases.
    *   `api/error.rs`: Transforms domain-specific errors (`DomainError`) into appropriate HTTP responses.
    *   `worker.rs`: A provisioning loop that wakes up periodically to process pending uploads, calling the same `service::provisioning` use case as the HTTP handlers.

*   **Driven Adapters**: These implement the capabilities (ports) that the core requires.
    *   `infra/db.rs`: Implements the `BuildingStore` port, providing persistence for building data using MongoDB.
    *   `infra/jobs.rs`: Implements the `UploadQueue` port, handling durable queuing of upload tasks with MongoDB.
    *   `infra/outbound.rs`: Implements the `DownstreamSync` port, managing outbound REST calls to other services.
    *   `service/fakes.rs`: An in-memory adapter for all three ports (`BuildingStore`, `UploadQueue`, `DownstreamSync`), used exclusively by the test suite to enable testing the core without a database or network.

*   **`main.rs`**: This file acts as the Dependency Injector, solely responsible for wiring each port (`service/ports.rs`) with its concrete implementation (e.g., `BuildingStore` with `MongoBuildings` from `infra/db.rs`).

.box {Three greps hold the boundary} type:{note}
    Cheap enough to run in CI, and concrete enough to be a governance check rather than a convention nobody enforces:

    - `domain/` names neither `axum` nor `mongodb` — the entity layer cannot learn about a framework by accident.
    - `service/` names neither of those nor `crate::adapters` — a use case cannot reach past its own ports.
    - No handler in `adapters/driving` names `crate::adapters::driven` — the driving adapter cannot skip the core and talk to a database directly.

    Each one fails loudly the moment the dependency rule is violated, which is the property a written convention does not have.

The payoff is testability that did not previously exist. The use cases are exercised against in-memory fakes of all three ports (`service/fakes.rs`) — the whole core suite runs with **no MongoDB and no network** — while the HTTP suite still drives the full `Router` via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` against a real MongoDB, the same code path a real request takes rather than a swapped-in double. Both matter: the fakes make failure paths (a refused downstream sync, a redelivered upload) reachable at all, and the HTTP suite proves the wiring is real. It also served as the characterization suite for the restructuring itself — every handler was rewritten without a single edit to it.

Metrics are recorded through an Axum `Router::layer`, which wraps the entire request/response cycle regardless of how the inner handler responds — so `http_requests_total`/`http_request_duration_seconds` increment for every route, labeled by `{method, route, status_code}` using the route template rather than the raw URL, so cardinality stays bounded even with real ids in the path. Cedar's policy bundle (`schema.cedarschema`, `policy.cedar`) is compiled into the binary via Rust's `include_str!` rather than read from disk at process startup — the production Docker image doesn't need `backend/auth-policy` present at runtime, only the builder stage does.

---

## Key Architectural Decisions

- **Server-owned identifiers.** Building identities are generated by the service, not accepted from the client, so the public identifier is decoupled from any client- or storage-specific id.
- **Tenancy from the token.** A building records the domains it belongs to; access is granted by matching those against the principal's memberships in the identity token — no per-request call to claims-gateway or tenancy-service.
- **Registration accepts, then provisions.** `POST /register` validates synchronously and answers `202` with a tracking handle; the twin itself is built by an in-process worker reading a durable queue. Validation stays ahead of the acknowledgement — a description the service would refuse must never be accepted — while everything that can only fail for reasons the caller cannot fix moves behind it. The caller polls the handle; delivery is at-least-once, so provisioning is written to converge on a second run rather than duplicate.
- **Two driving adapters, one use case.** The HTTP handler and the worker enter through the same `service::provisioning` module. Neither owns the behaviour, which is what stops the two paths drifting apart.
- **Best-effort downstream provisioning, with one exception.** Seeding the dependent contexts (default display preferences for distribution, per-room occupancy thresholds) is intentionally non-transactional — a failed seed is logged and swallowed. The sensor threshold clone is the exception: it fails the provisioning *job*, surfacing as `status: "failed"` on the handle rather than as a failed request (see the design note in [Building Management](../domain/building-management.qd)).
- **Structure treated as static.** Geometry and topology are modelled as stable; only metadata evolves. This keeps the spatial model dependable while live readings churn elsewhere.
- **Uniform geometry validation.** Every geometry-writing route — registration included — enforces the same position/dimension/capacity checks, returning `400` on a malformed room rather than storing bad data or surfacing an opaque `500`.

---

## Integration

- Owns `twin-db`; no other service reads it.
- Calls sensor-service and contracts-service to provision a new building — see [Communication & Data Flow](data-flow.qd).
- Exposes read-only building data to the assistant through an anti-corruption layer.

For the registration flow, name-normalisation rules, synchronisation details, and the API, see the [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd) internals page.
